<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  AUG-133 fixture: a document every RULE in auger.py accepts and the shipped XSD refuses.

  The requirement below has a stable id, the right `kind`, a lifecycle status, a version, a scope
  and a declared verification method — so `auger.py`'s own checks pass it. What it also carries is
  `severity="high"`, an attribute no block kind declares: the executable validator never looks at
  attributes it does not read, so before AUG-125/AUG-133 this corpus validated "ok" with exit 0
  while the normative grammar (specs/schema/auger-spec-1.0.xsd) rejected the document. That was the
  proof that the XSD was decorative: `auger spec validate` never loaded it.

  With the schema wired into the corpus loader this document is refused as
  `spec error (schema-invalid)`, naming this file and the undeclared attribute.
-->
<spec xmlns="urn:auger:spec:1" id="AUGER-SCHEMA-INVALID" version="1.0.0" schema-version="1.0">

  <requirement id="AUG-REQ-SCHEMA-1" kind="requirement" status="active" version="1.0.0"
               applies-to="auger.spec" verification="test" severity="high">
    Every block attribute is part of the grammar, so an attribute the grammar does not declare is a
    refusal rather than a silently ignored extra.
  </requirement>

</spec>
